-- title:
--   An mathcal O_D-linear isogeny of degree p^h divides [p^h]
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime and let $B$ be a Noetherian commutative ring in which the image of $p$ is nilpotent. Let $X$ and $Y$ be formal $\mathcal O_D$-modules over $B$ in the sense of `FormalODModule p B`: each consists of a commutative two-dimensional formal group law $F$ over $B$, a map $a \mapsto \operatorname{act}(a)$ from $\mathrm{Zp2}\,p = W(\mathbb F_{p^2})$ to pairs of power series in $B[[x_0,x_1]]$ and a further such pair $\varpi$, all of them endomorphisms of the law $F$, subject to $\operatorname{act}(1) = \mathrm{id}$, $\operatorname{act}(ab) = \operatorname{act}(a)\circ\operatorname{act}(b)$, $\operatorname{act}(a+b) = \operatorname{act}(a) +_F \operatorname{act}(b)$, $\varpi\circ\varpi = \operatorname{act}(p)$ and $\varpi\circ\operatorname{act}(a) = \operatorname{act}(\sigma a)\circ\varpi$ for the Witt-vector Frobenius $\sigma$. Let $\rho$ be a pair of power series which is $\mathcal O_D$-linear from $X$ to $Y$, i.e. a homomorphism of the underlying laws $X.F \to Y.F$ (vanishing constant coefficients and the substitution identity) commuting with $\operatorname{act}(a)$ for all $a$ and with the two $\varpi$'s, and let $h \in \mathbb N$ be such that $\rho$ has kernel of degree $p^h$: the quotient $B[[x_0,x_1]]/(\rho_0,\rho_1)$ is a finite projective $B$-module and for every field $\kappa$ and every ring homomorphism $f : B \to \kappa$ the analogous quotient $\kappa[[x_0,x_1]]/(f\rho_0, f\rho_1)$ has $\kappa$-dimension $p^h$. Then both components of $X.\operatorname{act}(p^h)$ lie in the ideal generated by the components of $\rho$, and there exists a pair of power series $\delta$ which is $\mathcal O_D$-linear from $Y$ to $X$ and satisfies $\delta\circ\rho = X.\operatorname{act}(p^h)$ and $\rho\circ\delta = Y.\operatorname{act}(p^h)$, where $p^h$ is the $h$-th power of $p$ in $W(\mathbb F_{p^2})$.
--
--   This is the formal-module incarnation of the fact that a finite locally free commutative group scheme is annihilated by its order: the kernel of $\rho$ is killed by $p^h$, so $[p^h]_X$ factors through $\rho$, yielding a quasi-inverse isogeny $\delta$ in the opposite direction. It is used in the rigidification step for fake elliptic curves in the Čerednik–Drinfeld part of the development, where isogenies of $p$-divisible formal $\mathcal O_D$-modules of bounded degree must be inverted up to $[p^h]$.
